   "source": {
    "label": "them-sampling",
    "number": "Theorem 1",
    "quote": "If \\textsf{SAU-Sampling} is run on a $K$-armed bandit problem ($K\\geq 2$) having arbitrary reward distribution $P_1, \\cdots, P_K$ with support in $[0,1]$, then its expected regret after any number $n\\geq \\max\\{\\frac{24\\log n}{\\min_a\\Delta_a^2}, 4\\}$ of plays is at most $$\\sum\\limits_{a: \\mu_a<\\mu_*}\\Delta_a\\left(\\frac{96\\log n}{\\Delta_a^2}+6\\right).$$"
   },
   "confidence": "medium",
   "notes": "Regret defined via means, so pseudo-regret. The algorithm uses no horizon, so this is recorded as anytime, but the bound only holds for n large enough. The proof assumes tau_a^2 <= 1, which Algorithm 1 does not enforce."
  },
